{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_09-22-17-omnipaxos-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor it in s['interpretations']:\n    if it['id']=='ebbec352695d43beb398bb1b239cda41-interpretation-0':\n        print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in it.items() if k in ('rationale','counterevidence','limitations')}, indent=1))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_09-22-17-omnipaxos-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor it in s['interpretations']:\n    if it['id']=='ebbec352695d43beb398bb1b239cda41-interpretation-0':\n        print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in it.items() if k in ('rationale','counterevidence','limitations')}, indent=1))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"rationale\": \"One index-space error in the StopSign accounting, five witnessed classes of consequence in nine receipts of one lineage, and the drift is neither bounded nor repaired inside the sealed configuration. Cause: sync_log adds the StopSign carried by a LogSync on top of sync_idx plus the suffix length while the sender's resume point may already count that StopSign, and create_log_sync never shifts sync_idx for a StopSign the sender already holds. (1) A replica that already accepted the configuration's StopSign and is given a resume point that covers it ends one index past its content: follower after reconnected() (9125b6a531234e7d9d2fa8cbb817df63 event 35), follower re-promising in an ordinary new term with no reconnected() call (6cdcff36336646859bffb9e8c3e39789 event 11), the leader that takes over from such a follower (dda5bb4179154e3abe90e69f5267b3ea event 13), and two followers under a snapshot-capable entry type (fd0b8be59c8f466fbf7768abb08f93b5 events 51, 52). Each such replica stops reporting its reconfiguration as decided and its decided-log read loses the entries (one element or nothing against six before). (2) The index drifts one step per re-synchronization round rather than settling: three consecutive reconnection rounds took the followers from 7 to 8 to 9 over unchanged content and the leader's decided index followed (6c879304cdde4ddd8e1640207cdbf94d events 41-49). (3) A write quorum of drifted reports makes the leader decide the reported index although it holds fewer entries (8b314551f1e049fcbf73a358f8ddf2c8 and 9125b6a531234e7d9d2fa8cbb817df63 event 24; snapshot-capable: fd0b8be59c8f466fbf7768abb08f93b5 event 53), after which the leader no longer reports the reconfiguration as decided and its decided log is unreadable. This decision needs two drifted reporters in a three-node cluster: with one, the leader stayed consistent (9125b6a531234e7d9d2fa8cbb817df63 event 37; 6cdcff36336646859bffb9e8c3e39789 event 9). (4) Both compaction paths inherit the drifted values and act on them: trim(None) took the minimum recorded accepted index, which had drifted above the leader's content, and compacted index 7 for a log of six indices, with the region then reported trimmed (ec3329dc273e452aa92bcd44652c116a events 9-15); snapshot(Some(8), false) was accepted inside the drifted decided index, advanced the compacted index to 8 and produced a snapshot holding none of the five decided entries the log contained, so the entries are no longer reachable and the snapshot that represents them is empty (e70b8baa087246c78ea3a64f24b8e97c events 0-5). Both calls returned success on input the documentation allows. (5) The drift neither saturates nor is repaired, and it does not crash the replicas (2993c16874454f86a3f8b04364a00ef4). From the healthy baseline of accepted 6 / decided 6 with a readable six-element decided log on all three nodes (events 9-11), eight further rounds of the same two-follower re-synchronization took both followers to accepted 14 / decided 14, one index per round with no saturation (events 12, 13, 18, 19, 33, 34), while the leader's own accepted index stayed 6 and its decided index followed to 14 with its decided-log read returning nothing in every round, from the first round on (events 14, 20, 35). A leader change was then performed onto node 1, one of the inflated followers: it took the lead in a new term and still reported accepted 14 and decided 14 instead of re-deriving its six indices of content (event 36), node 2 reported accepted 15 against decided 14 and node 3 -- the previously truthful leader, which held six indices -- was itself re-synchronized to accepted 7 (events 37, 38), so the drift spread to the replica that had stayed honest rather than being repaired. The three following rounds left the new leader at accepted 14 / decided 14 and took node 2 to accepted 16, 17, 18 and node 3 to accepted 8, 9, 10 while the decided index stayed at 14 (events 39-47): once no write quorum of recorded accepted indexes covers the further reports the decided index stops following, so the later drift appears as accepted indexes that never reach a decision, and the leader's decided index remains thirteen indices above the content it holds and its decided-log read remains unusable (node 2 read an empty decided log at events 45, 46, 47). No guarded drain, sample or leader change panicked over the eleven rounds (event 48: rounds 11, panics 0, 154 messages delivered). Comparisons 0-4 of that receipt are violated with witnesses 33, 34, 35 and 36 (the fifth additionally records nodes 2 and 3 as outside its applicability); comparisons 5-7 came out unknown because their declared admissions put a node condition on the prerequisite and a node-less identity on the panic event, so those states are asserted from the sampled events named above rather than from comparison outcomes. Controls: the pre-change samples and the no-reconnect control kept six indices of content, a six-element decided log, the StopSign at its own index and the reconfiguration reported as decided (8b314551f1e049fcbf73a358f8ddf2c8 events 9-11, 19-21; 6c879304cdde4ddd8e1640207cdbf94d events 38-40; fd0b8be59c8f466fbf7768abb08f93b5 events 48-50; 2993c16874454f86a3f8b04364a00ef4 events 9-11).\",\n \"counterevidence\": [\n  \"The no-reconnect control kept every endpoint consistent (accepted 6, decided 6, is_reconfigured true, six-element decided read: 8b314551f1e049fcbf73a358f8ddf2c8 events 9-11; 2993c16874454f86a3f8b04364a00ef4 events 9-11), and in the newterm case the new leader and the node that received nothing stayed consistent (6cdcff36336646859bffb9e8c3e39789 events 9, 10), so the deviation follows a re-synchronization whose resume point covers the StopSign.\",\n  \"The leader-side decision at a drifted index requires a write quorum of drifted reporters in one term (two of three nodes); one drifted reporter left the leader consistent (9125b6a531234e7d9d2fa8cbb817df63 event 37; 6cdcff36336646859bffb9e8c3e39789 event 9), and in the drift-bound receipt the decided index stopped advancing once the reports outran every write quorum (2993c16874454f86a3f8b04364a00ef4 events 46, 47 against decided 14).\",\n  \"The compaction consequences need the drift to cover every node the bound is derived from: trim's bound is the minimum recorded value, so a truthful replica would keep it honest (76cabb0efc3b4eda8eda8c9c79025550-interpretation-0), and the snapshot witness used a request strictly inside the drifted decided index because the StopSign equal case lowers the bound.\",\n  \"The documented reconfiguration flow expects a new instance once a node observes StopSign(ss, true), narrowing the window in which a user reads the old instance; the drift still hides that observation from the leader and, once compaction acts, removes the entries themselves.\",\n  \"The drift cannot be turned into new client values through the append path: append() refuses with PendingReconfigEntry while a StopSign is stored, which is the case throughout every observed drifted state, so the consequence set stays with index accounting, readability and compaction rather than with mis-assigned entries.\"\n ],\n \"limitations\": [\n  \"The drift was exercised for eleven rounds with one mid-run leader change; that range found no bound and no repair, but the decided index stopped following once the reports outran every write quorum, so how far the decided index can keep following is a quorum property that was observed with one three-node pattern rather than characterised in general.\",\n  \"Loss-triggered and timer-triggered re-synchronization were not exercised; the producers observed are explicit reconnected() calls and an ordinary leader change onto a node that had already drifted.\",\n  \"Only three-node clusters were used, with in-memory storage, no persistence across restarts and one snapshot-capable entry type; the compaction witnesses depend on that entry type's snapshot semantics.\",\n  \"Several comparisons in the repeated-rounds, trim, snapshot and drift-bound receipts came out unknown because their admissions shared event names across cases in one execution or put a node condition on the prerequisite; those states are asserted from the sampled operands, with the violated comparisons cited above.\",\n  \"The drift-bound case samples after each drained round, so it does not show intermediate states inside a round, and it does not exercise the forwarded compaction messages that a snapshot or trim call would send to peers.\",\n  \"The consequence set stays within index accounting, readability and compaction; the sealed configuration admits no further entries and refuses appends while the StopSign is stored, so no value is mis-assigned.\"\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
